  "query_preview": "..(Saturday night through Thursday)\nIssued at 409 AM EDT Fri Oct 23 2026\n\nPersistence forecasting prevails in the initial stages of the long \nterm forecast period, but change is now visible on the horizon. The \nantecedent ridging pattern peaks on Sunday, when a 588-590 dam ridge \naxis will stretch up the Mississippi River Valley and into the Lower \nOhio River Valley. In other words, the ridge axis will be positioned \nimmediately upstream of the CWA in Western Kentucky. Such positioning \nwill keep Eastern Kentucky in a regime of dry northerly flow aloft, \nand the related subsidence should sustain mostly clear skies for as \nlong as the pattern persists. Expect efficient diurnal radiation and \nmixing processes to dominate the sensible weather forecast as a \nresult. So, overnight ridge/valley temperature splits (ridges in the \n40s and valleys in the 30s), nocturnal valley fog/frost formation, \nand seasonably warm/sunny afternoons were all maintained in the \nforecast grids through Monday afternoon. \n\nThe ridging pattern is progged to break down beyond then, although \nmodels resolve the evolution of this pattern shift quite \ndifferently. Confidence is high that the ridging will flatten from \nMonday night into Tuesday. The flow aloft will shift to a more \nwesterly and then southwesterly orientation in this time frame, \nleading to modest midlevel moisture return and a gradual increase in \nclouds. Sky cover should peak on Wednesday morning as a shortwave \ntrough propagates towards the region, but this is not accompanied by \nany meaningful precipitation chances. Ensemble probabilities for \nmeasurable (>0.01 inches) precipitation remain below the 15% \nthreshold through midweek. This can largely be attributed to the \nlack of meaningful low-level warm/moist air advection out ahead of\nthe system's arrival, but forecast confidence begins to decrease \nbeyond then. Some guidance keeps a regime of deeper west- \nsouthwesterly flow in place over the region as the initial \ndisturbance deamplifies and phases with broader troughing digging \ninto the Midwestern CONUS. Other model solutions show shortwave \nridging building into the area late next week, followed by a \npotential Halloween weekend storm system. The former solution \nwould favor increased atmospheric moisture, greater sky cover, and\nthus slight cooler temperatures. While the latter camp of \nsolutions would initially favor warmer and drier weather, there \nwould be an opportunity for deeper SW flow to build into the area\non the backside of that ridging feature. If that deeper SW flow \nregime materialized, the approach of the next upper atmospheric \ndisturbance around Halloween could introduce more meaningful \nprecipitation chances to the forecast. So, interests with outdoor\nspooky season plans are encouraged to monitor for forecast \nupdates in the coming days, as guidance will likely come towards a\nconsensus by early next week.",
